    "howl": {
      "word": "HOWL",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To utter a loud, protraced, mournful sound or cry, as dogs and wolves often do. And dogs in corners set them down to howl. Drayton. Methought a legion of foul fiends Environ'd me about, and howled in my ears. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To utter a sound expressive of distress; to cry aloud and mournfully; to lament; to wail. Howl ye, for the day of the Lord is at hand. Is. xiii. 6.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To make a noise resembling the cry of a wild beast. Wild howled the wind. Sir W. Scott. Howling monkey. (Zoöl.) See Howler, 2. beasts. Deut. xxxii. 10.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To utter with outcry. \"Go . . . howl it out in deserts.\" Philips.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "The protracted, mournful cry of a dog or a wolf, or other like sound.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A prolonged cry of distress or anguish; a wail.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
